Q1. What is regression?
Regression is a machine learning technique used to predict continuous numerical values, such as house prices, salaries or taxi fares.
Q2. Which dataset did you use?
I used the Uber fare dataset (uber.csv) to predict taxi fares.
Q3. What is the target variable?
The target variable is fare_amount, which represents the Uber ride fare.
Q4. What is Linear Regression?
Linear Regression is a supervised machine learning algorithm that predicts a numerical value by finding a relationship between input features and the target variable.
Q5. What is the difference between classification and regression?
- Classification predicts categories, such as Yes or No.
- Regression predicts numerical values, such as price or fare.
  Q6. What is Ridge Regression?
  Ridge Regression is Linear Regression with L2 regularization. It reduces large coefficients to help prevent overfitting.
  Q7. What is Lasso Regression?
  Lasso Regression is Linear Regression with L1 regularization. It can reduce some coefficients to exactly zero and perform feature selection.
  Q8. What is the difference between Linear, Ridge and Lasso Regression?
  | Linear | Ridge | Lasso |
  |---|---|---|
  | No regularization | L2 regularization | L1 regularization |
  | May overfit | Controls large coefficients | Can make coefficients zero |
  | Uses ordinary least squares | Shrinks coefficients | Performs feature selection |
  Q9. What is overfitting?
  Overfitting happens when a model performs very well on training data but performs poorly on new, unseen data.
  Q10. What is underfitting?
  Underfitting happens when a model is too simple to understand the patterns in the data.
  Q11. Why do we calculate distance between pickup and drop-off locations?
  To estimate how far the Uber ride travels. Distance can help the model predict the fare.
  Q12. What is Euclidean distance?
  Euclidean distance is the straight-line distance between two points.
  Formula: \(d=\sqrt{(x_2-x_1)^2+(y_2-y_1)^2}\)
  Note: If longitude and latitude are used directly, this is a coordinate-space approximation, not an accurate distance in kilometres.
  Q13. What is IQR?
  IQR stands for Interquartile Range. It is used to identify outliers.
  Formula: \(IQR=Q3-Q1\)
  Q14. What is an outlier?
  An outlier is a value that is unusually high or low compared with most other values in a dataset.
  Q15. Why do we remove outliers?
  We remove incorrect or extreme values because they can negatively affect model training and prediction.
  Q16. What is correlation?
  Correlation measures the relationship between two variables. Its value ranges from -1 to +1.
- +1: Perfect positive relationship.
- -1: Perfect negative relationship.
- 0: No linear relationship.
  Q17. What is a heatmap?
  A heatmap is a graphical representation of data using different colours. In this practical, it displays correlations between numerical features.
  Q18. What is train_test_split?
  It divides the dataset into training and testing data. The training data is used to train the model, and testing data is used to evaluate it.
  Q19. What does test_size=0.2 mean?
  It means 80% of the data is used for training and 20% for testing.
  Q20. What is random_state=42?
  It ensures that the data is split in a reproducible way each time the code runs.
  Q21. What is R² score?
  R² measures how well the regression model explains the variation in the target variable. A higher value generally indicates a better fit.
  Q22. What is MSE?
  MSE stands for Mean Squared Error. It calculates the average of squared differences between actual and predicted values.
  Q23. What is RMSE?
  RMSE stands for Root Mean Squared Error. It is the square root of MSE and measures prediction error in the target variable's units.
  Q24. What is MAE?
  MAE stands for Mean Absolute Error. It calculates the average absolute difference between actual and predicted values.
  Q25. What is the difference between MSE, RMSE and MAE?
  | Metric | Meaning |
  |---|---|
  | MSE | Average squared error |
  | RMSE | Square root of MSE |
  | MAE | Average absolute error |
  For all three error metrics, a lower value is generally better.
  Q26. What is actual vs predicted graph?
  It compares the actual fare values with the fares predicted by the model. Points closer to the ideal diagonal line indicate better predictions.
  Q27. What is a coefficient in Linear Regression?
  A coefficient represents how much the predicted fare changes when an input feature increases by one unit, assuming other features remain constant.
  Q28. Why do we use Ridge and Lasso?
  We use them to reduce overfitting and improve model generalization.
  Q29. What is the difference between fit and predict?
- fit() trains the model using training data.
- predict() generates predictions using the trained model.
  Q30. Why do we predict a new Uber ride?
  To check whether the trained model can estimate the fare for new ride details that it has not seen before.
