Q7. What is SVM?
SVM stands for Support Vector Machine. It is a supervised machine learning algorithm used for classification and regression.
Q8. How does SVM work?
SVM finds the best decision boundary, called a hyperplane, that separates different classes with the maximum possible margin.
Q9. What is a hyperplane?
A hyperplane is a decision boundary that separates different classes in a dataset.
Q10. What are support vectors?
Support vectors are the data points closest to the decision boundary. They help determine the position of the hyperplane.
Q11. What is margin in SVM?
Margin is the distance between the decision boundary and the nearest data points from different classes. SVM tries to maximize this margin.
Q12. What is a kernel in SVM?
A kernel is a function that helps SVM handle data that cannot be separated easily using a straight-line decision boundary.
