Q1. What is clustering?
Clustering is an unsupervised machine learning technique used to group similar data points together.
Q2. What is K-Means Clustering?
K-Means is an unsupervised machine learning algorithm that divides data into K different clusters based on similarity.
Q3. Which dataset did you use?
I used the Iris dataset (Iris.csv).
Q4. How many records are present in the Iris dataset?
The standard Iris dataset contains 150 records.
Q5. How many features are present in the Iris dataset?
There are four numerical features: Sepal Length, Sepal Width, Petal Length and Petal Width.
Q6. What are the three species of Iris flowers?
- Iris-setosa
- Iris-versicolor
- Iris-virginica
  Q7. Is K-Means supervised or unsupervised?
  K-Means is an unsupervised learning algorithm because it does not require labelled data to create clusters.
  Q8. What is a cluster?
  A cluster is a group of data points that are similar to each other.
  Q9. What is a centroid?
  A centroid is the centre point of a cluster. It is calculated by taking the mean of the feature values of all points in that cluster.
  Q10. What is K in K-Means?
  K represents the number of clusters we want to create.
  Q11. Why did you use K=3?
  Because the Iris dataset contains three known flower species, and we selected three clusters for our experiment.
  Q12. How does the K-Means algorithm work?
1. Select K initial centroids.
2. Calculate the distance between data points and centroids.
3. Assign each point to its nearest centroid.
4. Recalculate the centroids.
5. Repeat until the clusters stop changing significantly.
   Q13. What is Euclidean distance?
   Euclidean distance measures the straight-line distance between two data points.
   Formula: \(d=\sqrt{(x_2-x_1)^2+(y_2-y_1)^2}\)
   Q14. What is WCSS?
   WCSS stands for Within-Cluster Sum of Squares. It measures the total squared distance between data points and their assigned cluster centroids.
   Q15. What is the Elbow Method?
   The Elbow Method is used to find a suitable number of clusters by plotting WCSS against different K values.
   Q16. Why is it called the Elbow Method?
   Because the graph looks like an elbow at the point where the decrease in WCSS starts becoming slower.
   Q17. What happens to WCSS when K increases?
   WCSS generally decreases as the number of clusters increases because data points become closer to their assigned centroids.
   Q18. What is inertia_ in K-Means?
   inertia_ gives the WCSS value of the fitted K-Means model.
   Q19. What is n_clusters=3?
   It tells the K-Means algorithm to create three clusters.
   Q20. What is init="k-means++"?
   It is a method used to select better initial centroids, helping K-Means produce good clusters.
   Q21. What is random_state=42?
   It makes the random initialization reproducible, so we can get consistent results when running the code again.
   Q22. What is n_init=10?
   It runs K-Means with 10 different centroid initializations and keeps the result with the lowest inertia.
   Q23. What is labels_?
   It stores the cluster number assigned to each data point.
   Q24. What is cluster_centers_?
   It stores the final centroid coordinates of all clusters.
   Q25. Why do we remove the Id column?
   The Id column is only an identifier and does not represent a meaningful flower measurement for clustering.
   Q26. Why do we use fit_predict()?
   It trains the clustering model and returns the cluster label assigned to each data point.
   Q27. What is the difference between classification and clustering?
   | Classification | Clustering |
   |---|---|
   | Supervised learning | Unsupervised learning |
   | Uses labelled data | Does not require labels |
   | Predicts known classes | Finds groups in data |
   | Example: Spam detection | Example: Customer grouping |
   Q28. Why do we use a scatter plot?
   A scatter plot helps visualize the data points and understand how they are distributed among different clusters.
   Q29. Why do we plot centroids?
   We plot centroids to show the centre position of each cluster.
   Q30. What is the main disadvantage of K-Means?
   We must choose the number of clusters in advance, and the algorithm can be affected by outliers and initial centroid selection.
   Q31. What is feature scaling?
   Feature scaling brings numerical features to a comparable scale. It is useful for K-Means because K-Means uses distance calculations.
