Q1. What is Reinforcement Learning?
Reinforcement Learning is a type of machine learning in which an agent learns by interacting with an environment and receiving rewards or penalties.
Q2. What is Q-Learning?
Q-Learning is a reinforcement learning algorithm that learns the best action to take in a particular state.
Q3. What is an agent?
An agent is the learner or player that takes actions in an environment.
Q4. What is an environment?
An environment is the place where the agent performs actions. In this practical, the Tic-Tac-Toe game is the environment.
Q5. What is a state?
A state represents the current situation of the game board.

Q6. What is an action?
An action is a move made by the agent. In Tic-Tac-Toe, selecting an empty cell is an action.

Q7. What is a reward?
A reward is feedback given to the agent after performing an action. It helps the agent learn good and bad actions.

Q8. What is a Q-table?
A Q-table stores the Q-values of different state-action pairs. It helps the agent select better actions.

Q9. What is the purpose of the Q-table in this practical?
It helps the agent remember which moves are better in different board situations.

Q10. Why do we use NumPy?
NumPy is used to create and manage the Q-table efficiently.

Q11. What is the size of the Q-table?
The Q-table has 19683 rows and 9 columns because there are \(3^9 = 19683\) possible board states and 9 possible cell positions.

Q12. Why do we use np.zeros()?
It creates a Q-table filled with zero values initially.

Q13. What is the learning rate?
The learning rate controls how much new information affects the existing Q-value. In this practical, it is 0.9.

Q14. What is the discount factor?
The discount factor determines the importance of future rewards. In this practical, it is 0.9.

Q15. What is the exploration rate?
The exploration rate controls how often the agent tries random moves instead of choosing the best-known move. In this practical, it is 0.3.

Q16. What is exploration?
Exploration means trying new or random actions to discover better moves.

Q17. What is exploitation?
Exploitation means choosing the action with the highest known Q-value.

Q18. What is the difference between exploration and exploitation?
Exploration tries new actions, while exploitation selects the best-known action.

Q19. What is the purpose of the reset() function?
It resets the game board to an empty state before starting a new game.

Q20. What is the purpose of get_available_moves()?
It returns the positions on the board that are empty and available for playing.

Q21. What is the purpose of make_move()?
It places the player's mark in the selected empty position.

Q22. What is the purpose of check_win()?
It checks whether a player has completed three marks in a row, column, or diagonal.

Q23. How many winning combinations are possible in Tic-Tac-Toe?
There are 8 winning combinations: 3 rows, 3 columns, and 2 diagonals.

Q24. What is a terminal state?
A terminal state is the final state of a game, such as a win, loss, or draw.

Q25. What is a draw?
A draw occurs when all nine cells are filled and no player has won.

Q26. What is an episode?
An episode is one complete game, starting from an empty board and ending with a win or draw.

Q27. What is np.argmax() used for?
It returns the index of the maximum value. It can be used to select the action with the highest Q-value.

Q28. What is np.random.choice() used for?
It randomly selects an action from the available moves.

Q29. What is the Q-Learning formula?
\[
Q(s,a) = Q(s,a) + \alpha[r+\gamma\max Q(s',a')-Q(s,a)]
\]

It updates the Q-value using the reward and the estimated future reward.

Q30. What does alpha represent in the formula?
Alpha represents the learning rate.

Q31. What does gamma represent in the formula?
Gamma represents the discount factor.

Q32. What is the purpose of get_state_index()?
It converts the Tic-Tac-Toe board into a unique numerical index so that the Q-table can store its values.

Q33. Why do we use mark + 1 in state conversion?
The board contains -1, 0, and 1. Adding 1 converts them into 0, 1, and 2 for base-3 encoding.

Q34. What is the purpose of evaluate_agents()?
It runs multiple games between agents and counts their wins and draws to evaluate their performance.

Q35. What is the main objective of this practical?
The main objective is to demonstrate how an agent learns to make better decisions in Tic-Tac-Toe using Q-Learning.
